# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- attributes
ATTRIBUTES: dict[str, str] = {
    "ATR002": "The unit, such as kilograms or litres, in which the quantities on this link between a production run and a batch it used are stated. It applies to the quantity the recipe called for and to the quantity actually used.",
    "ATR006": "How much of a batch was built into a unit, in the unit of measure stated for that batch. It is the only place that quantity is kept.",
    "ATR008": "What the outside manufacturer is contracted to do for a product, such as making it, packing it or both, and under which specification it works.",
    "ATR009": "The person who fitted the machine into the place. It says who to ask about how the machine was installed on the day it went in.",
    "ATR010": "The capacity in which the person took part in the recorded event, such as operator, checker, decider or releaser. It is what shows, for a decision or a release, who made it.",
    "ATR011": "How much of the batch the production run actually used, in the unit of measure stated on the link. Set against the quantity the recipe called for, it shows how far the run departed from the recipe.",
    "ATR012": "How much of the batch the production run actually produced, in the unit of measure stated for it. It is the quantity the batch is followed by when it is split or sent on.",
    "ATR013": "How the machine failed when it stopped, in words. It is empty when the stoppage was planned and the machine did not fail.",
    "ATR014": "Whether the business checked what the supplier's document says about the batch by a test of its own. Where it is false, the claim is the supplier's and is not reported as the business's own result.",
    "ATR015": "How long the batch was exposed to conditions outside those its place must keep, counted in hours. It shows how serious the exposure was.",
    "ATR017": "What a product is to the organisation, such as a raw material, a pack component or a finished unit. It decides whether the product may be bought, made, used in other products or sold.",
    "ATR018": "Whether the organisation buys the product in finished, so that what is inside it is known only from the supplier's declaration.",
    "ATR029": "The lot code given to the batch when it was first packed or transformed. It is carried unchanged at every later movement, so it is how the batch is followed back and forward.",
    "ATR030": "The date the batch was made. For a batch that is split or combined, it is the date the new batch came into being.",
    "ATR032": "How much of the product the batch holds when it is made, in the product's unit of measure. Later splits are checked against it.",
    "ATR033": "How much of the batch this part holds, in the same unit as the batch. The parts of a batch add up to what was divided.",
    "ATR048": "The date the machine was built or the unit was made. It shows how old the machine or unit is.",
    "ATR049": "The identifier itself, such as a serial number or an asset number, exactly as the maker or the organisation wrote it. It is how a machine or unit is matched to outside records.",
    "ATR050": "What was measured when a reading was taken of the machine, such as its temperature or the hours it has run.",
    "ATR051": "The number the reading gave, in the unit the measure is normally stated in. Read with the time of the reading, it shows how the machine was running.",
    "ATR052": "What happens when the machine fails in this way, such as lost output, spoiled product or a risk to safety. It helps judge how much the failure matters.",
    "ATR053": "A whole number showing how much the machine matters to production and to safety, where a higher number means a failure matters more. It is empty for an individual unit of product.",
    "ATR054": "How far down the plant structure the place is, such as site, area, production line, storage zone or work unit. It tells a reader where in the hierarchy the place sits.",
    "ATR057": "Why one product may not follow another on the line, such as an allergen that must not carry over. It is the reason the rule exists.",
    "ATR058": "Whether a clean is needed on the line between the two products. Where it is true and no clean is recorded between the runs, the line was run against the rule.",
    "ATR060": "What the step is called, in the words the factory uses, such as pasteurising or filling.",
    "ATR061": "What sort of thing the step needs, such as a kind of machine, a qualified person or a material.",
    "ATR062": "The floor for a setting: the lowest value the step may be run at and stay in control. A reading below it means the step ran too low, and it is stated in the unit the setting is normally read in.",
    "ATR063": "The ceiling for a setting: the highest value the step may be run at and stay in control. A reading above it means the step ran too high, and it is stated in the same unit as the floor.",
    "ATR064": "What the setting is set to at this level of the recipe. For the instructions issued to a batch, it is what the operator was told to set.",
    "ATR065": "Which level the recipe is written at: the general recipe, the site recipe, the master recipe or the control recipe issued for one batch.",
    "ATR066": "The date the recipe at this level was issued. It shows which instructions were in force when a batch was made.",
    "ATR067": "How often a sample is taken at the step, such as every pallet or every hour.",
    "ATR068": "What the method is called in the factory, such as the usual way of making a product on a particular line.",
    "ATR069": "What the place is called, in the words the business uses for it, such as a plant name, a line number or a cold store.",
    "ATR070": "The identifier itself, such as a site number or a registration number, as the issuer wrote it. It is how the place is matched to a supplier's or a regulator's records.",
    "ATR071": "Who issued the identifier, such as a regulator, a customer or the organisation itself. The same number from two issuers can mean two different places.",
    "ATR072": "The lower limit for a place: the coldest or lowest reading at which stock is safe to keep there. If the place falls below it, the stock was held out of condition.",
    "ATR073": "The upper limit for a place: the warmest or highest reading at which stock is safe to keep there. If the place rises above it, the stock was held out of condition and a breach is recorded.",
    "ATR074": "The date the person was awarded the qualification. Work done before this date was not done by a qualified person.",
    "ATR075": "The date the qualification stops being valid. Work done after this date, unless the qualification was renewed, was not done by a qualified person.",
    "ATR076": "The time the person's shift began. Together with the shift pattern, it shows who was on duty at any moment.",
    "ATR077": "What the person meant by signing, such as that they did the work, checked it or released it. The same name on a record means different things for different meanings.",
    "ATR078": "The time the person signed the record. It is the time of signing, which may be later than the time the work was done.",
    "ATR079": "Whether the signing is the check of another person's work, as is required where a step must be verified by a second qualified person, rather than the work itself.",
    "ATR080": "What the assessment covered, such as which materials or which site of the supplier or outside manufacturer.",
    "ATR081": "The date the assessment stops being valid. A delivery received after this date was not from a supplier holding a valid assessment.",
    "ATR082": "What the supplier answered when asked to put a problem right. It is empty until the supplier replies.",
    "ATR083": "Which property of the batch the supplier's document states, such as its moisture level or whether it contains an allergen.",
    "ATR084": "What the document states the property to be. It is the supplier's claim, and it is read together with whether the business checked it.",
    "ATR085": "What kind of movement of goods it is: a purchase, a receipt, an issue to production, a transfer between places or a despatch.",
    "ATR086": "The time the goods moved. For a movement that takes time, it is the time they left or arrived, as the kind of movement says.",
    "ATR087": "Whether the goods become the organisation's when they are received, rather than when they are paid for or when they leave the supplier. It decides who owns goods that are in transit.",
    "ATR088": "How much of the item moved on the line, in the item's unit of measure.",
    "ATR089": "The condition the held stock is in, such as available, on hold or rejected. Only stock that is available may be counted as available.",
    "ATR090": "How much of the item was held at the place in that condition when it was counted, in the item's unit of measure.",
    "ATR091": "The time the stock was counted. A figure is true only as at this time.",
    "ATR092": "The time the event happened. For an event that lasts, it is the time it started.",
    "ATR093": "The time the production run began on the line, which is when the first input was taken for it. Together with the time it ended, it gives how long the batch took to make.",
    "ATR094": "The time the production run ended on the line, which is when the last output was taken from it. It is empty while the run is still going on.",
    "ATR095": "How much more or less of its inputs the run used than the recipe called for. It is worked out from the quantity specified and the quantity actually used for each input.",
    "ATR096": "Why the batch was divided, such as to send part for rework, to fill different packs or to hold part back for testing.",
    "ATR097": "How long the changeover took, counted in minutes from the end of one product to the start of the next.",
    "ATR098": "Whether the changeover included cleaning the line. Where it is true, the line was cleaned between the two products.",
    "ATR099": "Why production stopped, chosen from a governed list so that stoppages can be counted and compared by reason.",
    "ATR100": "The time production stopped. Together with the time it started again, it gives how long the stoppage lasted.",
    "ATR101": "The time production started again. It is empty while the stoppage is still going on.",
    "ATR103": "The method used to test the sample, as written in the specification or as the standard method named.",
    "ATR104": "What the test found, as the person who did it recorded it, in the units the method gives. Whether it met the specification is held beside it.",
    "ATR105": "Whether the result met the specification it was judged against. Where it is true, the result was within the limits.",
    "ATR106": "How serious the finding is, such as critical, major or minor, as the quality system grades it.",
    "ATR107": "The time the problem was contained so that no more affected product could go on. It is empty while the problem is still uncontained.",
    "ATR110": "What was decided to do with the failed material: use it as it is, rework it, regrade it, scrap it or return it.",
    "ATR111": "Why that decision was taken, in the words of the person who took it. It is what a later reader relies on to understand why failed material was kept, reworked or scrapped.",
    "ATR112": "The cause found for the problem, which the action is meant to remove. It is the cause the action is judged against when its effect is checked.",
    "ATR113": "The date the business checked that the action worked. An action closed before this date was closed without a check.",
    "ATR114": "The date the action was closed. It is empty while the action is still open.",
    "ATR115": "What the release was based on, such as the test results and records that were looked at before the batch was let go.",
    "ATR116": "The time the batch was released. Before this time the batch is not available for use or sale.",
    "ATR117": "Which batches and which markets the recall covered, as announced. The batches actually reached are found through the links between batches.",
    "ATR118": "What the person reported about the product, in their own words, exactly as they gave it. It is not altered by what the investigation later finds.",
    "ATR119": "Why the earlier record was corrected, in the words of the person who made the correction. The earlier record itself is kept as it was.",
    "ATR120": "The time the stock first went outside the conditions its place must keep. Together with the length of the exposure, it shows how serious the breach was.",
    "ATR121": "How much of the input the recipe called for, as it stood when the batch was made. It is kept with the run so that a later recipe does not rewrite what was asked of this one.",
    "ATR122": "Whether the run used an alternative to the input the recipe named. Where it is true, the run did not use the specified input.",
    "ATR123": "Which level of packing it is: a single unit, an inner pack, a case, a layer or a pallet.",
    "ATR124": "The number by which the level is identified when it is counted and moved, such as the number on a case label.",
    "ATR125": "How many of the next level down one of this level holds, such as the number of units in a case. It is always above zero, and a level never holds itself.",
    "ATR126": "What was done to the machine, such as the part replaced or the adjustment made.",
    "ATR127": "Whether the item is a machine standing in a place or an individually identified unit of product.",
    "ATR128": "Which version of the specification the result was judged against, so that the judgement stays true when the specification is later issued again.",
    "ATR129": "Whether the finding is a departure from procedure or a failure against specification. The two are never recorded as one.",
    "ATR130": "What the way of failing is called, such as a seized bearing or a leaking seal.",
}

